<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thracians</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Thracians were an ancient Indo-European people of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, living in the lands of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, European <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>. Divided into many tribes, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odrysians">Odrysians</a>, Getae and Bessi, they traded with the Greek colonies of the coast and were famed for their horsemen, gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> work and tombs. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> in 46 CE, and their language died out in late antiquity.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thracians.jpg" alt="Thracians" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ann Wuyts · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:National_Archaeological_Museum_Sofia_-_Bronze_Head_from_the_Golyama_Kosmatka_Tumulus_near_Shipka.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
